WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Genghis Khan, Conquests of Love and War.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:26.840
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:29.180
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.280
It's just us.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:40.560
Yes, just us and Koei, who insists on porting their grand strategy games to the NES with their third entry in the...

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.000
Is this a franchise? They're all like different franchises.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.140
I'm going to call it a franchise.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:53.280
It seems to be based on like the same systems and bones, and it's the same people, so yeah.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.540
It's just funny because like Romance of Three Kingdom got like 13 games.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.240
Nobunaga's ambition is going to get a sequel.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.260
I believe Genghis Khan is going to get a sequel.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.920
So then are those sequels also part of this franchise?

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.320
This is like very confusing stuff.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.740
All right, so let's not call it a franchise.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.100
Let's call it a family.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.580
Yeah. You got...

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:21.920
It's like, you know, how there are a bunch of Hardys and there's also a bunch of Carl's Juniors, but they are basically the same.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.520
I think that's how that would work.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.160
I thought Hardys and like...

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.360
Oh, no, wait. I'm thinking of Rally's and Checkers.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.600
Maybe I'm thinking of Rally's and Checkers.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.560
Yeah, I've never been to...

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.300
I've actually only ever been to Checkers out of the four we've listed.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:37.680
Is Carl's Juniors any good?

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.560
I don't know. I've never been there.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.840
Hmm. You went to California recently, In-N-Out Burger?

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.080
In-N-Out Burger is a place I've been to.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:47.440
It's good. That's good.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.660
I've had that. I've liked it.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.400
I like burgers, mostly.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.580
Burgers, especially on the fast food variety.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:02:07.660
But you know what? Fast food and these kinds of games are very similar because whether you go to McDonald's, Burger King, or even something fancy like In-N-Out where it's like, oh, we're exclusive to the West Coast.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:08.920
Like, come find us.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:13.480
No matter where you go, you're getting the same burger, right?

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:14.700
It's got all the same elements.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:26.940
Yeah. And yes, the cheeseburger mostly tastes the same except for like, you know, the patented bun strategy of like how much sugar they're allowed to put into their buns that somehow stay well.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.220
Yeah, exactly. Yeah.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:39.820
And that's what Koei's doing here is like, their slogan is, they supply the past and you make the history, but they're just supplying you the past piecemeal.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.500
Like, they're saying like, okay, now play as the Mongols.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.120
Okay, now play as China.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:45.340
Now play as Japan.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:50.140
They're not giving us the past all at once and they're not really doing much to change it.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:56.480
So whether I'm in Japan or China or now with the Mongol Empire, I don't know.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.020
I'm not really feeling the differences.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:15.660
Yeah, I mean, there's some base level mechanics that are changing here based on whatever game we've played that sort of fits with the theme of the, of whatever time period they're trying to cover.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:27.380
Uh, I don't really, I, I, I can't really focus on which ones like romance did that was different than, uh, that was different than Nobunaga.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.400
But this one seems to have changed the most.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:39.900
And at this level, it's almost like we're trying to tell the difference between like Madden games from year to year because their systems are so similar.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.680
Yeah, that's a good point, Sean.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:55.840
Um, maybe a more like apt or gaming, um, analysis would have been like the, the monopoly games that are, uh, you know, every single one is like, oh, they just changed what boardwalk is right to like whatever that franchise or IP is.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:05.580
But then occasionally you get one that has like in the manual, if you bother to read it, it's like special fun rules that like they try to tie into the IP.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.240
So this way it's more like that.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.320
And it's like, no one really reads that.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:12.440
So you could just play it the same way and not notice any of the differences.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:22.020
But if you do go the extra step and find out that like, oh, in the, in the Simpsons version, it does this, but in the New York Mets monopoly version, it does this.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:24.400
That's not true. By the way, I don't think either one of those do anything different.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:25.340
I own both.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:32.640
Um, you know, I'm playing the same, I'm playing the same game both times.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:39.940
And that's kind of what I feel like regardless of like the little changes, but there is is boardwalk just Mike Piazza as like a person.

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:40.980
It's just like him.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:45.200
I think it's actually, I think it would be Shea stadium at the time, which makes sense, right?

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.360
You make the actual stadium that you play at the final destination.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.660
Mike Piazza is probably like in the greens, you know, the greens are valuable.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:05:00.900
All right. Genghis Khan though does make changes though, Sean, and you're right.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:16.060
I'm not, I'm not fully giving it the credit that it's due because it actually adds, um, unlike what Nobunaga was doing, where it was like, Hey, you can play as like six of the countries, uh, six of the territories, or you can play as all 50 something of the territories.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:25.700
This one actually takes the time to be like, no, the two modes are actually going to be something kind of different where like Mongo conquest, which starts in like the year 1175.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:33.920
Um, that's like a one player mode where you must play as a specific Lord and conquer a specific set of territories.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:43.360
And it's not, it's not super linear or anything, but you know what to expect and can get better at playing that very specific version.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:51.420
Yeah. Whereas like world conquest or any of the other, you know, Nobunaga's ambition or romance of three kingdoms, that's more of the grand strategy.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:53.740
Like anything can possibly happen.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:57.320
There's no way to keep track of like what country 35 is doing.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.280
You just gotta, you know, you just gotta hope that when you get there, you're the stronger army.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:08.620
I wouldn't say that mongol conquest is any easier than world conquest, but it definitely is smaller in scope.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:15.220
So it can almost be seen as like a dry run or as just like a tutorial.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:27.660
Yeah. And I'll be honest, that's the only mode I really checked out for this game because it was a little more accessible to the point where this is the first time I was able to go on game FAQs and actually get a,

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:34.740
a guide that I could understand and like sort of mimic and yeah, the turns wouldn't work out exactly the same way.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:42.280
So it wasn't like I was playing someone else's game verbatim, but at least I had a good starting point of like, all right, make treaties with these guys,

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.500
build your army for this size.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:07:02.480
Like that's great because that's the kind of thing you can't anticipate in world conquest because you don't know what the surrounding armies are going to even like be what their base stats are going to be or what kind of army they're going to turn out to be or whether they're even going to focus on building an army and whether you could just steamroll them later.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:04.820
Like that's hard to find out in the world conquest mode.

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:13.640
Yeah, I think again, one of my biggest problems with this series has been how opaque the stats are.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:25.300
And I know that we played three of them and you'd think that I'd have enough like time with them by now that I can kind of get some relationships between stats that just make sense.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:26.980
But I still am not there.

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:41.620
But what I would say, like what I mean by that is like, I don't know how many troops is enough to think, okay, I can go to war now because like 50 something might look like a high number to me.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:47.680
But then I'll go to war and it's like, oh, he's already got like 2000 troops or something.

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:58.660
But what I will say about Mongol conquest is the map is a lot easier to digest and kind of like keep a mental image in your head.

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:05.200
Like you can see that there's sort of like a choke point in territories right in the middle.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:19.560
So you you kind of want to consolidate on one side before you move over to the other just to like minimize the amount of lines in battle you might have or enemies that want to like attack your flank.

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:25.980
So I will say that it's easier to think tactically on the grandest scale in Mongolian conquest.

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:41.080
And also you're able to make three decisions on on the kinds of things you want to do with your empire per term, which is great because, you know, in the past, it's like everybody makes a move and then everybody makes a move.

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:44.520
This time, it's almost like, you know, first, I'm going to train my troops.

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:47.580
Then I'm going to try to sign a treaty with these people.

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:50.120
And then if they don't sign it, I'm going to freaking fight them.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:55.540
You know, like you don't have to wait all those turns to find out what's going to happen next.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:09.860
Yeah, it it also kind of because when it's just one move per turn turn order, I feel like becomes a problem because you can only do one thing, obviously.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:20.000
So if you just if by some for some reason, country 12 goes before country 13 country 12 has an advantage.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:28.700
Now that you can kind of just like weight your priorities, you can do the three things that you meant to do in those three cycles.

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:31.580
But now they're all happening concurrently.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:33.700
And you can think a bit more.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:39.320
To me, it just feels a bit more leveled out because of that.

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:48.600
You made me think of and I don't know if the original risk board game does this, but certainly the computer version of risk that I played growing up did.

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:54.740
And more famously, the board game diplomacy, which can only ruin friendships, never restore them.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:04.880
Yes, those those games have a way so that like you decide your moves, the computers or fellow opponents decide their moves.

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:07.260
And then they're all executed like at once.

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:15.540
And there's some contradictions that have to be like worked out or some conflicts that need to be fought because, you know, you chose to fight this army.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:16.820
But then this army invaded you.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:18.560
And it's like, well, that's what you decided.

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:19.960
You know, like those kind of thing work out.

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:30.140
It is interesting that like, you know, for a thing that's trying to like simulate history, the idea of like going in turns doesn't necessarily like it doesn't work.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:32.660
And at the very least, it should it should probably be random.

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:39.340
The turns like after each turn should be like a random drawing of the like who goes next after everybody's gone.

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:47.020
Yeah, and I guess in a single player game, it may as well be sort of like that concurrent execution.

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:51.600
And then whatever gets priority is random.

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:56.640
But because like, you know, the computer, you can't see what the computer is doing.

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:00.380
So it might have incomplete information on you.

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:02.060
You have incomplete information on them.

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:15.600
But when you get to like the four player game, when you're in World Conquest, then that's kind of when that would take a take a bigger it would cause a larger issue, I guess.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:20.940
And because these games, especially from Koei, are treated like historical fiction.

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:40.860
Is there a case for the historical nonfiction or rather historically accurate mode where like everybody else other than you does behave as expected and you can just choose to, you know, use that history knowledge to either watch it unfold or take advantage of it and rewrite history?

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:53.980
Well, I know that in some paradox games, it's a lot harder in something like in something like Crusader Kings when there are hundreds of agents.

00:11:54.520 --> 00:12:04.340
But in something like Hearts of Iron, which is just a simulation of World War Two, there's only like a hundred or so different countries that have their own AI.

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:23.760
There's a checkbox before you start a game that you can either check or uncheck that determines whether the AI will behave as they did in real life to the best of its ability, you know, considering how you change your behavior as opposed to the actual country.

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:26.620
Or you can just have it be random.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:33.420
And yeah, I really don't know if any of that is in this game.

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:45.860
Like if if the AIs have predetermined routines that you are interacting with or if everyone just sort of has the same personality across the board, I'm not really sure.

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:54.440
And it'd be interesting if, you know, in history, territories five and six, which are not actually they're not actually next to each other.

00:12:54.540 --> 00:13:02.220
That's another thing, you know, just real quick, because we were talking about this in our Clash of Demon Head episode that like the numbers for the routes weren't concurrent.

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:04.320
So it wasn't like one, then two, then three.

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:06.980
It's also like that in this map.

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:13.360
Like, why not just make it so that it just goes from left to right, you know, territory one, two, three, four.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:15.660
Instead, it's like they're kind of near each other.

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:16.800
It's like serpentine. They don't touch.

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:22.880
Yeah. It starts from like one corner and then it goes to the left and then to the right and to the left.

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:37.180
It can be confusing because then it'll be like this country has has ordered you to give this plot of land to them or or to make them a vassal.

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:52.120
And by the time I'm at the screen that is telling me that this country is telling me to do this, I don't know who's actually threatening me because it's just giving you a number and I don't have the map memorized.

00:13:53.260 --> 00:14:03.280
Yeah. And then to round out my point, there is just that if it was historically, I agree, like territories five and six to had like a peace agreement in real life that like, yeah, they shouldn't fight in the game.

00:14:03.640 --> 00:14:07.960
Or at least there should be a checkbox for that, like what you were suggesting with Hearts of Iron.

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:25.160
Yeah. Now, the arguably the easiest way to win this game and there is no easy way to win this game, but it would be to master the thing that has annoyed me the most about this game ever since Nobunaga's ambition and the thing they really refuse to fix and fix in a meaningful way.

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:29.940
I'm not talking about like, yeah, they make quality of life improvements, but they need to revamp the combat in general.

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:47.940
And the combat in this game is something that like if you understand it and you know how to use the numbers to your advantage and where to be on those maps and how to fight, you're going to steamroll the opponents because it's all about, you know, just having a larger army and conquering smaller territories to eventually conquer everything.

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:50.820
That's where I struggle with this game.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:58.200
I feel like even though it's close to a turn based strategy game, it's not that and I can't do that.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:00.900
So, well, I think that that's the easiest solution.

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:09.800
A lot of people would argue also that there's this whole market manipulation thing that you have to understand and that that's the real way to win where like, you know, once you get to spring,

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:14.280
that's the time to sell off all your items and get a lot of gold.

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:29.600
And then when you get to winter, that's when you buy back everything you need for the following season so that this way you always have exactly what you need and can anticipate the, you know, the quantity of your army, your people, your taxes and so on and so forth.

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:33.060
You know, that's more exciting to me than the combat.

00:15:33.260 --> 00:15:35.500
And that means that something's probably wrong with the combat.

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:53.120
Yeah, well, okay, let me just again describe, I think, in a bit more articulate way what I don't understand about like the overarching economic and like overview tactical level of this game.

00:15:54.300 --> 00:16:06.560
In any other strategy game like this, I kind of get the gist of how to scale and how to get that positive feedback loop of growth going.

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:26.000
But in this, I like I went to war, I beat the guy, took his land, and now I've got a barely defended now two plots of land that I guess I have to wait till the next year to get more troops unless I want to hire some more.

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:30.620
But my gold reserves are down because I used it all to fight the war.

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:32.920
And I guess I can sell some food.

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:40.000
But then again, I don't know how much is going to be taken during the next season.

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:43.320
So I don't want to mess with that too much.

00:16:43.320 --> 00:17:06.060
So you end up in a way where like, you know, in something like Civilization or like Crusader Kings again, or one of these paradox games, you can kind of have an idea of how to extrapolate how to get from where your resources are currently to where they are in the future.

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:11.940
And I just had, I was just the chicken with my head cut off.

00:17:12.100 --> 00:17:13.380
I didn't know what I was doing with it.

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:23.340
The combat, while it is jank, I at least understood what like what attrition would be walking through woods.

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:33.660
And yeah, the like the eight directions or the six directions you can go choosing between those is kind of wonky.

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:35.680
But I understood what you're supposed to do.

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:39.760
And if you end up next to an enemy, you won't be able to move any further.

00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:46.340
And I understood that different areas had different offensive and defensive buffs.

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:48.280
And I like I got that.

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:50.780
That was understood to me.

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:52.640
Everything else was not.

00:17:53.140 --> 00:18:09.780
Arguably the most interesting aspect of the Mongol conquest thing, if you can get to it, is that once you do conquer every territory, then, you know, a screen presents you with the information that the real battle is about to begin.

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:17.440
And you just load into the world conquest mode, but with your stats from the Mongol conquest mode.

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:31.880
So I think like that is that's a real like, whoa moment, you know, like an aha thing to happen to somebody who manages to get far enough in Mongol conquest to then import their save into the grander game.

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:32.880
That's just crazy.

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:45.780
Yeah, I mean, I I had a weird feeling of just like, oh, this is like Spore, but actually impressive when you get to that that threshold.

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:50.440
Because, you know, in Spore, you go from, oh, you're just a you're just a thing.

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:52.540
And then, oh, now you're a tribe.

00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:54.100
And now you're a nation state.

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:57.080
And now you're the whole world.

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:00.900
And just that expansion of scope over time.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:02.320
I like that idea.

00:19:02.560 --> 00:19:14.080
And I've always like, you know, when I was a kid, I'd be like, oh, it'd be really cool if like the Sims tied into Sim City tied into like a nation running game.

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:15.360
Like, that'd be so cool.

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:17.260
As a simulation.

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:19.220
I knew it wasn't really feasible.

00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:29.360
But when you when you nail it down to just like, OK, consolidating the Mongol Empire to then becoming the Mongol Empire and trying to conquer the world.

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:34.560
I guess when you when you nail it down a bit more, you can actually implement that.

00:19:34.940 --> 00:19:47.440
Right, right. And then, you know, the equivalent for that would be if there was a way to hook up like two NESs so that I had Genghis Khan in mind and you had Romance of the Three Kingdom in yours.

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:52.980
And then I was able to take my final file from Genghis Khan and your final file from Romance of the Three Kingdoms.

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:56.520
And we duke it out based on our empires.

00:19:56.780 --> 00:19:58.580
Like, that would be what that is.

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:02.260
And I think that's the kind of stuff that makes like, you know, we're not supposed to talk about these games.

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:08.800
But like Sonic the Hedgehog 3 and Knuckles, like the fact that it does open up and then you put the other cartridge on top.

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:13.000
Like, wow, what a time to be alive for something like that to happen.

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:15.280
And that's not even like a grand strategy game.

00:20:15.400 --> 00:20:17.780
That's just a platform that gets some extra levels.

00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:19.480
No, that is funny.

00:20:19.540 --> 00:20:20.620
I didn't make that connection.

00:20:21.080 --> 00:20:24.000
You know, you have to assess if that's worth it, though, right?

00:20:24.160 --> 00:20:28.840
Like playing all of Mongol Conquest and then saying like, yeah, no, I haven't had enough.

00:20:29.020 --> 00:20:31.840
I'll definitely keep going and now conquer the world.

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:37.900
Like, I guess you have to be Genghis Khan himself to really have that kind of thirst, right?

00:20:38.020 --> 00:20:39.660
To say, I'm going to keep going.

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:57.940
